(2657077, 362426, 9906, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>I really love lighthouses!  This one looks very cool!  I love the perspective that you chose for this one.  In shots such as this often the perspective makes the building look wrong.  You managed to make this look very natural.  I really like the palm tree leaning into the shot.  It adds to the drama of the shot.  I also think the contrast of the dark doorway and the white of the lighthouse adds to the drama of the shot.<br/> <br/>What could make this better?  Well the first thing I see that detracts a little from the shot are the palms next to the door.  A slightly different camera angle may have been possible to keep all the elements in the shot but removing those trees.  I see a possible reshoot in your future!  I am really dissapointed that you didn''t have a dramatic sky day for this shoot.  There are a few clouds in this sky but as is they are almost a distraction.  Another reason to revisit!<br/> <br/>Why didn''t this score better?  While this is a cool perspective, it''s not all that unique.  I think that was the point of the challenge.<br/> <br/>I can''t wait to see if you have more shots of this local.<br/> <br/>Yours<br/>TC</td>', 'From the Critique Club: I really love lighthouses!  This one looks very cool!  I love the perspective that you chose for this one.  In shots such as this often the perspective makes the building look wrong.  You managed to make this look very natural.  I really like the palm tree leaning into the shot.  It adds to the drama of the shot.  I also think the contrast of the dark doorway and the white of the lighthouse adds to the drama of the shot. What could make this better?  Well the first thing I see that detracts a little from the shot are the palms next to the door.  A slightly different camera angle may have been possible to keep all the elements in the shot but removing those trees.  I see a possible reshoot in your future!  I am really dissapointed that you didn''t have a dramatic sky day for this shoot.  There are a few clouds in this sky but as is they are almost a distraction.  Another reason to revisit! Why didn''t this score better?  While this is a cool perspective, it''s not all that unique.  I think that was the point of the challenge. I can''t wait to see if you have more shots of this local. YoursTC', '2006-07-25 01:09:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2657218, 360293, 9906, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>First of all I would like to say that I always like critiqueing/helping out the younger photographers here at DPC.  I love it when someone with no training to hinder them can take shots that blow away those of us that think they know what they are doing.<br/> <br/>This is actually a very nice shot!  I love the contrasts that you have going here.  Nature versus man, new versus old, color versus cold b/w, the angled plant versus the straight line of the pipe, I could go on and on...  If this was a contrast challenge this probably would have scored much better!  However, this was a challenge based on perspective where the unique perspective wins.<br/> <br/>There is nothing technically that I can say badly about this shot.  Everything is in great focus.  Everything is exposed properly.  The composition though not ''great'' still follows enough of the ''rules'' that it still has staying power.  I can look at this shot 2 weeks from now and still appreciate it!  The rules that you did break (eg:  There is nothing truly white in what is mostly a black and white shot) really don''t matter.<br/> <br/>In short, I wish I had the perspective (no pun intended) of youth to see things without being limited by the ''rules''!  Keep shooting.  Don''t get discouraged.  If you ever have any questions, this is a great community of people to get help from!<br/> <br/>Yours<br/>TC</td>', 'From the Critique Club: First of all I would like to say that I always like critiqueing/helping out the younger photographers here at DPC.  I love it when someone with no training to hinder them can take shots that blow away those of us that think they know what they are doing. This is actually a very nice shot!  I love the contrasts that you have going here.  Nature versus man, new versus old, color versus cold b/w, the angled plant versus the straight line of the pipe, I could go on and on...  If this was a contrast challenge this probably would have scored much better!  However, this was a challenge based on perspective where the unique perspective wins. There is nothing technically that I can say badly about this shot.  Everything is in great focus.  Everything is exposed properly.  The composition though not ''great'' still follows enough of the ''rules'' that it still has staying power.  I can look at this shot 2 weeks from now and still appreciate it!  The rules that you did break (eg:  There is nothing truly white in what is mostly a black and white shot) really don''t matter. In short, I wish I had the perspective (no pun intended) of youth to see things without being limited by the ''rules''!  Keep shooting.  Don''t get discouraged.  If you ever have any questions, this is a great community of people to get help from! YoursTC', '2006-07-25 02:10:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2657512, 362268, 19959, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>Hi Barbara,<br/> <br/>This is a nicely exposed and well captured photo. I like it.<br/> <br/>I think perhaps it was not an ideal choice for this challenge as it does not scream ''perspective'' (Which a large number of voters need to hear during voting!)<br/> <br/>You did not receive many comments during this challenge which may suggest that voters slipped over the image too quickly, possibly clicking the middle of the road ''5'' and moving on. This is a shame as this shot has many merits. It is sharp and has great imapact.<br/> <br/>How might it be improved...<br/>IMO the crop is a little too tight, I would like to see either more of the swimmer and pool or go the other way and crop in even tighter to the face.<br/> <br/>The light is harsh, but this works in this circumstance. As for fringing, well this can be removed in post-processing, but in basic editing not really an option.<br/> <br/>I have looked at your previous photos and I am impressed by your approach. There is a choice on DPChallenge IMO to either go with your own style or tailor shots for the voters. In your case stick with your style as it shows great promise.<br/> <br/>This shot and your success shot show good technique and only scored low because of the impression of not conveying the challenge sufficiently.<br/> <br/>Overall very good work, this is your highest scoring entry deservedly and I look forward to seeing more or your pictures. I think very soon you will be seeing scores in the 6''s and this leads in turn to top 20''s, Top 10''s and ribbons.<br/> <br/>Regards<br/> <br/>Chris<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club. Hi Barbara, This is a nicely exposed and well captured photo. I like it. I think perhaps it was not an ideal choice for this challenge as it does not scream ''perspective'' (Which a large number of voters need to hear during voting!) You did not receive many comments during this challenge which may suggest that voters slipped over the image too quickly, possibly clicking the middle of the road ''5'' and moving on. This is a shame as this shot has many merits. It is sharp and has great imapact. How might it be improved...IMO the crop is a little too tight, I would like to see either more of the swimmer and pool or go the other way and crop in even tighter to the face. The light is harsh, but this works in this circumstance. As for fringing, well this can be removed in post-processing, but in basic editing not really an option. I have looked at your previous photos and I am impressed by your approach. There is a choice on DPChallenge IMO to either go with your own style or tailor shots for the voters. In your case stick with your style as it shows great promise. This shot and your success shot show good technique and only scored low because of the impression of not conveying the challenge sufficiently. Overall very good work, this is your highest scoring entry deservedly and I look forward to seeing more or your pictures. I think very soon you will be seeing scores in the 6''s and this leads in turn to top 20''s, Top 10''s and ribbons. Regards Chris ', '2006-07-25 04:54:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2657608, 361015, 16636, '<td valign="top">Ian - great shot and processing!</td>', 'Ian - great shot and processing!', '2006-07-25 06:50:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(2657795, 362352, 12448, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Okay, interesting image that has some potential.<br/> <br/>Not having a clue what the original looks like, I don''t know if you did any cropping or not.  As mentioned before the centering of the pole doesn''t really work for this one, if you had used the rule of thirds instead?  Moving the pole to the left and having more lines on the right.  <br/> <br/>Also, the use of the power lines, with the right cropping and composition you could have them leading you in and out, coming from right to left would be different than most but I think it would work for this image.  Have the line come in at the upper corner and out in the lower corner.  Try to see if you can get one line to come in exactly on the corner.<br/> <br/>The sky is rather dull, being basic editing, there''s not a lot we can do about that except adjust your hue/saturation in blue and make it pop a bit more.<br/> <br/>I like the pole being dark but so is the sky in comparison so you don''t really get that element that makes it stand out like silhouette &lt;sp?&gt;, it just blends in a bit right now.<br/> <br/>But overall not a bad idea for the challenge, just a bit more work and this could really make an interesting and outstanding photo.<br/> <br/>I hope my comments help and Good Luck in future Challenges!<br/> <br/>Deannda</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Okay, interesting image that has some potential. Not having a clue what the original looks like, I don''t know if you did any cropping or not.  As mentioned before the centering of the pole doesn''t really work for this one, if you had used the rule of thirds instead?  Moving the pole to the left and having more lines on the right.   Also, the use of the power lines, with the right cropping and composition you could have them leading you in and out, coming from right to left would be different than most but I think it would work for this image.  Have the line come in at the upper corner and out in the lower corner.  Try to see if you can get one line to come in exactly on the corner. The sky is rather dull, being basic editing, there''s not a lot we can do about that except adjust your hue/saturation in blue and make it pop a bit more. I like the pole being dark but so is the sky in comparison so you don''t really get that element that makes it stand out like silhouette <sp?>, it just blends in a bit right now. But overall not a bad idea for the challenge, just a bit more work and this could really make an interesting and outstanding photo. I hope my comments help and Good Luck in future Challenges! Deannda', '2006-07-25 09:27:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2657900, 362357, 37961, '<td valign="top">I love the coloration and angle. The kids riding by really helps to add complexity to the image. Great catch...</td>', 'I love the coloration and angle. The kids riding by really helps to add complexity to the image. Great catch...', '2006-07-25 10:15:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(2659103, 362382, 62373, '<td valign="top">same as everyone ellse <b>SMALL</b></td>', 'same as everyone ellse SMALL', '2006-07-25 15:57:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(2659497, 361974, 43340, '<td valign="top">Wow that really is quite amazing work!  Fantastic photos and basic use of image editing.</td>', 'Wow that really is quite amazing work!  Fantastic photos and basic use of image editing.', '2006-07-25 18:35:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2659507, 361885, 43340, '<td valign="top">I''m not being critical because this is a great photo and is teriffic use of the fisheye lens, but for those of us who enjoy airline photography (i.e. airliners.net) this kind of technique is quite often used with similar results.  But I still like what you''ve done.</td>', 'I''m not being critical because this is a great photo and is teriffic use of the fisheye lens, but for those of us who enjoy airline photography (i.e. airliners.net) this kind of technique is quite often used with similar results.  But I still like what you''ve done.', '2006-07-25 18:39:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(2659735, 361956, 54702, '<td valign="top">I think it was the dashboard being reflected in the window that really ruined this shot.  I did appreciate the comments though.  Thanks everyone.</td>', 'I think it was the dashboard being reflected in the window that really ruined this shot.  I did appreciate the comments though.  Thanks everyone.', '2006-07-25 21:20:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(2660089, 362441, 12448, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Wow!  I love this shot!  It takes me back to when I used to fly with my dad in his Eurocoup!  That was so much fun!  And to capture it as it was taking off.  The motion blur is wonderful on the houses while the plane is so clear.  Well done.  <br/> <br/>The first thing I noticed though was the pinkish/orange tint on the wings of the plane.  Now reading the title that explains to me why they are like that but for some reason it doesn''t quite fit with the rest of the shot.  Maybe if you were behind the plane and one could see the sunset in the background but then you lose the motion blur on the houses.   Sadly a lot of people put points towards the title as well and that might be part of what happened.<br/> <br/>Secondly, I''m sure you got a few DNMC votes as well.  If you were taking the shot from inside the plane or even from behind it again, giving one more the idea of taking off and the vision ahead. <br/> <br/>I wonder how much you brightened this if you did because again, the glow of the sunset just doesn''t seem to mesh with the light in the background, I can''t quite explain why but it doesn''t.  Being a basic challenge you could take the hue/saturation and desat the yellow and red just a bit to tone down the color on the wings and then bump the contrast just a touch, a tiny touch.  Maybe then it will seem more natural.<br/> <br/>But I do like it, it does remind of one of the few good memories I do have with my dad, thank you for that.<br/> <br/>I hope my comments help and Good Luck in future Challenges!<br/> <br/>Deannda<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Wow!  I love this shot!  It takes me back to when I used to fly with my dad in his Eurocoup!  That was so much fun!  And to capture it as it was taking off.  The motion blur is wonderful on the houses while the plane is so clear.  Well done.   The first thing I noticed though was the pinkish/orange tint on the wings of the plane.  Now reading the title that explains to me why they are like that but for some reason it doesn''t quite fit with the rest of the shot.  Maybe if you were behind the plane and one could see the sunset in the background but then you lose the motion blur on the houses.   Sadly a lot of people put points towards the title as well and that might be part of what happened. Secondly, I''m sure you got a few DNMC votes as well.  If you were taking the shot from inside the plane or even from behind it again, giving one more the idea of taking off and the vision ahead.  I wonder how much you brightened this if you did because again, the glow of the sunset just doesn''t seem to mesh with the light in the background, I can''t quite explain why but it doesn''t.  Being a basic challenge you could take the hue/saturation and desat the yellow and red just a bit to tone down the color on the wings and then bump the contrast just a touch, a tiny touch.  Maybe then it will seem more natural. But I do like it, it does remind of one of the few good memories I do have with my dad, thank you for that. I hope my comments help and Good Luck in future Challenges! Deannda ', '2006-07-25 23:51:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2661858, 359913, 28742, '<td valign="top">Technically an excellent photo, but for the challenge, it is just not interesting or compelling enough, IMO.</td>', 'Technically an excellent photo, but for the challenge, it is just not interesting or compelling enough, IMO.', '2006-07-26 05:18:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(2663537, 359913, 56797, '<td valign="top">I like this. great color and technically done very well. I think the border works here but i''m not a good judge of that. overall borders are not well recieved in challenges. Its sort of soothing rather than interesting but never the less a great photo.</td>', 'I like this. great color and technically done very well. I think the border works here but i''m not a good judge of that. overall borders are not well recieved in challenges. Its sort of soothing rather than interesting but never the less a great photo.', '2006-07-26 12:49:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(2664032, 362286, 18349, '<td valign="top">I thought this was an interesting image...I like the noise and the overall sense of impending doom.  ;)  I agree that the foreground is a bit large, and would like to see more roof...but overall there is a good emotive quality to it.  :)</td>', 'I thought this was an interesting image...I like the noise and the overall sense of impending doom.  ;)  I agree that the foreground is a bit large, and would like to see more roof...but overall there is a good emotive quality to it.  :)', '2006-07-26 14:40:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(2664265, 362343, 50578, '<td valign="top">I never got around to looking at this challenge.  So I didn''t vote on any of the photos or see any for that matter til today. <br/>Had I voted I would have scored this an 8<br/> <br/>I think this is a very nice photo.  I used to have freckles like that when I was a kid.  <br/> <br/>The only thing I see that may have hurt your score  and I really would hope it had nothing to do with it. the typo on Model Modle...  You will get your nit pickers..   Other than that.  There really is no way to explain  the voters here. It is like  trying to figure out why birds fly into glass windows  or why dogs bark at moon.</td>', 'I never got around to looking at this challenge.  So I didn''t vote on any of the photos or see any for that matter til today. Had I voted I would have scored this an 8 I think this is a very nice photo.  I used to have freckles like that when I was a kid.   The only thing I see that may have hurt your score  and I really would hope it had nothing to do with it. the typo on Model Modle...  You will get your nit pickers..   Other than that.  There really is no way to explain  the voters here. It is like  trying to figure out why birds fly into glass windows  or why dogs bark at moon.', '2006-07-26 15:46:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(2664328, 362343, 38267, '<td valign="top">Wonderful image...look at those cute freckles :)</td>', 'Wonderful image...look at those cute freckles :)', '2006-07-26 16:05:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(2664479, 362211, 40679, '<td valign="top">I like the PoV, but your entry doesn''t scream "perspective" to me. Do like the compo, allthough it could be a bit too tight on the left.<br/>I really like the black tule-fabric-stuff-thing-prop!</td>', 'I like the PoV, but your entry doesn''t scream "perspective" to me. Do like the compo, allthough it could be a bit too tight on the left.I really like the black tule-fabric-stuff-thing-prop!', '2006-07-26 16:44:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(2664498, 362238, 40679, '<td valign="top">this is just beautiful! the sky looks fantastic, and the buildings are very dramatic like this.<br/> <br/>congrats on the top 10!</td>', 'this is just beautiful! the sky looks fantastic, and the buildings are very dramatic like this. congrats on the top 10!', '2006-07-26 16:48:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2664674, 362380, 40679, '<td valign="top">wow, didn''t see this one! this looks majestic, great job Waz! hope  you had a great time in NYC!</td>', 'wow, didn''t see this one! this looks majestic, great job Waz! hope  you had a great time in NYC!', '2006-07-26 17:24:42.000000', NULL, 0),
